      "writtenOn": "2025-10-17T00:08:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve only contributed some drive-by fixes to `git-cl.py`\u0027s formatting functionality, so I don\u0027t think I\u0027m a great reviewer for this. Moving myself to CC.\n\nI made some general comments from a quick skim of the code.",
      "revId": "734f1a6355f92238fcd5626238da6073a5da63da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4cfcc174_4cc611a2",
        "filename": "deps_format.py",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1394418
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-17T00:08:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This seems to be quite error-prone and assumes a specific format of the input. I looked into it and it seems like the file format is generally \"Python\", as DEPS files are `exec`\u0027d: https://crsrc.org/c/buildtools/checkdeps/builddeps.py;l\u003d124;drc\u003df4ee964fb1d71dae4c7243ac78c76949c95906e5\n\nWhile I don\u0027t have a super strong opinion - an imperfect formatter is much better than no formatter - have we considered using an AST-based approach instead? For example, could we start off using the existing formatter used for Python files, maybe with a different configuration?",
      "revId": "734f1a6355f92238fcd5626238da6073a5da63da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "de314948_9eaee2e6",
        "filename": "deps_format.py",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1394418
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-17T05:50:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "After doing a bit of research, I think LibCST codemods (https://libcst.readthedocs.io/en/stable/codemods_tutorial.html) are probably the way to go if we wish to do something like this. There\u0027s an example in Chromite: https://crsrc.org/c/third_party/chromite/contrib/libcst_tool.py",
      "parentUuid": "4cfcc174_4cc611a2",
      "revId": "734f1a6355f92238fcd5626238da6073a5da63da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1f070b18_5da75a27",
        "filename": "deps_format.py",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1147264
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-17T17:22:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e For example, could we start off using the existing formatter used for Python files, maybe with a different configuration?\n\n+1. Chromium uses YAPF to format python files. If DEPS needs custom styles, I\u0027d consider crafting .style.yapf for DEPS. If it turns out that yapf isn\u0027t the best tool for DEPS formatting, I\u0027d consider black, which is available in depot_tools.\n- https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/tools/depot_tools/+/main:black\n\n\u003e I think LibCST codemods are probably the way to go if we wish to do something like this. There\u0027s an example in Chromite:\n\nThanks for the suggestion. It looks like a great alternatives for the tree parser, if we have to implement the formatter. However, I\u0027d lean on using an existing Python formatter, if possible, due to maintenance costs and style tuning support.",
      "parentUuid": "de314948_9eaee2e6",
      "revId": "734f1a6355f92238fcd5626238da6073a5da63da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "347d3ad4_3374e7d2",
        "filename": "deps_format.py",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1394418
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-20T23:34:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Existing Python formatters do not have the capability of reordering strings in a list, as they are mostly intended for keeping the source semantically identical.\n\nI assume the \"keep this list of (dependency) strings sorted\" was the main motivation for this CL, so that\u0027s why I suggested the AST-based approach.\n\nhttps://github.com/google/keep-sorted could work here, but I don\u0027t think it has a nice way of running it locally and may be error-prone. There is a Gerrit check for \"keep-sorted\" comments, though.\n\nhttps://github.com/bazelbuild/buildtools/tree/main/buildifier, intended to be used for Starlark could also work for this using `# keep sorted` comments (https://github.com/bazelbuild/buildtools/blob/2eb4fccefb52eeea5cbae46bd145afc0dc625ad5/build/rewrite.go#L515), but getting it running in Chromium might be difficult due to it being written in Go. I\u0027m not too sure how well it handles `Var` calls, though.",
      "parentUuid": "1f070b18_5da75a27",
      "revId": "734f1a6355f92238fcd5626238da6073a5da63da",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
